# Eclipse design

The Eclipse plugin `fearlessPluginProject` of Controllers shows the projects of a manager inside Eclipse, with content assist. The manager, its requests and its files are in ControllersDesign; the content of `.fearless_out` is in CoordinatorDesign.

## Eclipse

Connect Eclipse asks for a file or a folder through a file picker. The Eclipse folder is the one folder holding `.eclipseproduct`; the chosen file or folder is the Eclipse folder, is directly inside the Eclipse folder, is directly inside a folder directly inside the Eclipse folder, or is a folder holding only the Eclipse folder.
Connect Eclipse puts the plugin in the Eclipse folder, with `manager.info` next to the plugin: `manager.info` holds the path of the manager program folder, as a `taggedText`. From that path the plugin finds the manager program, the manager folder and the base cache (see CoordinatorDesign).

The Output of a running program has no bound on its size, so the manager does not pass it through files: Eclipse listens on a port, and the `Run` and `Compile` of Eclipse carry that port.
Eclipse shows a refused `Run` or `Compile` only through the dialog of the manager: the Output view stays empty.
Eclipse follows the mains or the compilation of the last `Run` or `Compile` of Eclipse; Eclipse sends no other `Run` or `Compile` until those mains or that compilation end, except for a save. Everything else Eclipse shows (the error of the last compilation, the unit test log) is read from files.

Saving a file of a code project in Eclipse compiles the project:
- while Eclipse follows a compilation of that project, the save is ignored: the files stay unsaved in the editors;
- otherwise Eclipse sends `Terminate` of the project (a running main is ended), saves every open file of the project together, and sends `Compile` of the project, or `Check` for a code project Eclipse sees as 'invalid', as Build does. During a compilation of that project that Eclipse does not follow, the manager refuses that `Terminate` and that `Compile` or `Check`, Eclipse saves the files anyway, and the project is 'out of date' once the running compilation ends (see `.compile` in CoordinatorDesign).

The error file `_error.txt` is a 'stale error file' once the project folder, a file or folder of the project whose path holds no file name or folder name starting with `.`, the folder `.config`, the folder `.config/icon` or a file of `.config/icon` has a time after the time of the error file and not after the current time. A 'stale error file' is shown nowhere.
The plugin names each registered project by the first top level `.fearless` file of its folder in file name order, and mirrors no folder without one.
The plugin mirrors every registered project into the Eclipse workspace, in the Fearless perspective only, and writes into a project folder only the files and folders the user saves or creates; a project the manager forgets is removed from the workspace. The mirror of the project `hello` is the workspace project `fearless_hello`; a workspace project `fearless_hello` that is not the mirror of `hello` is an error. The folder of the Eclipse workspace is not inside a registered project folder or the manager folder, and holds none of them: otherwise the plugin shows the error naming that folder, and mirrors no project. Every two seconds the plugin reflects `projects.info` and the `.fearless_out` folders: the error of `_error.txt` (see CoordinatorDesign) in the Problems view, on the file and at the line the error names, or on the project while that file does not exist or the error names no file, while `_error.txt` is not a 'stale error file', and in the JUnit view the unit test logs written in `.out/logs` since the start of the last `Run` of that project that Eclipse follows, one test suite per log (the newest unit test log, before any such `Run`): the complete records of the log, the lines of the log up to the last newline, without a test case whose closing line is not written yet. The plugin shows the followed mains or compilation in the Output view, emptied when the mains of a `Run` or a compilation start, like the Output area of the manager: a view with no buttons, where a stack frame and the header of a compile error are links opening that file at that line. Fearless files show with syntax highlighting. Build (`Compile` for a code project that is not 'invalid', `Check` for any other), Clean (`DeleteCache`), the main button below, the kind switches of the Fearless menu of a project, New Fearless Project (`Register`), Show in manager (`Show` with the name of the project) and Forget project are messages to the manager.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds the main button of the manager for the project of the selection, or, when the selection names no file or folder of a mirrored project, of the active editor. The main button shows what pressing the main button does, each with an image of its own: Check, Compile, Run, Run V, or Terminate while Eclipse follows the mains of a `Run` of that project, and is greyed out while Eclipse follows a compilation of that project. Run V asks which mains to run, with the selected mains checked, then sends `SelectMains` naming the mains chosen and `Run`. Besides the main button, the toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds only the buttons of text editing (Save, Undo, Redo, Search, the toggles of the editor and the navigation in the text); a button that does nothing for Fearless or repeats another is hidden, and so is the Run menu, in the Fearless perspective only. Eclipse finds the state from the files, with the rules of an 'autoCheck': the kind in `projects.info`, the marker, and the rules of `Coordinator.check` (see CoordinatorDesign). A code project 'invalid' for a cause Eclipse does not read shows the button of the state Eclipse finds, and the manager refuses that request with the 'problem' of the project.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective also holds New Package and New Type, for the project of the active editor or of the selection; each asks for a path inside the project folder and refuses a path that is not valid, naming the rule of the Fearless file names the path breaks:
- New Package takes a path like `lib/_beer`, and makes the package folder `_beer` with the folders holding the package folder;
- New Type takes a path like `lib/_beer/point`, and makes the file `point.fear` holding the type `Point`. The option Rank file makes the rank file of that package instead, `_rank_app.fear` or the rank chosen, holding a `use` line for each of `base.Main`, `base.UnitTests`, `base.Test`, `base.Void`, `base.Bool`, `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float`, `base.Str`, `base.List`, `base.Lists`, `base.Opt`, `base.Opts`, `base.Block`, `base.Error` and `base.Debug`. The option Example tests adds a test suite checking some arithmetic.
New Folder and New File of the Fearless perspective warn when the name is not a valid file name of a Fearless project, naming the rule the name breaks, and create the folder or the file anyway. New Folder is in the toolbar next to New Package and New Type, for the folder of the active editor or of the selection, and greyed out as they are when no file or folder of a mirrored project is selected or open.
New Fearless Project registers a folder, made if missing (`Register`). New Fearless Project can first write a demo into a new or empty folder, then make the project code (`Kind`) once the manager lists the project: Hello world; Simple GUI, a window with a button; Shortcut and OpenWith, a main started by the shortcut file of the main and a main claiming an extension, each with an icon of its own. The OpenWith main of this demo is a window showing the text of the file the main is opened with; every file dropped on that window adds the text of that file at the end.
A missing or malformed file of the manager is an error shown in Eclipse, and the plugin stops following the manager until Eclipse starts again.

## Content assist

Content assist works in a Fearless file of a package folder of a mirrored project, after a dot and on request. Content assist uses the compiled information of the last compilation of the project and of the base cache: the `.types` of the `<pkg>.json` of every package, the `<pkg>.txt` of the package of each proposal (see CoordinatorDesign), and the map directives of the rank files of the project. A malformed `<pkg>.json`, a missing `<pkg>.txt`, and a public type or a method of a type missing from the documentation are errors: the content assist popup shows the error, with one proposal, Clean, which sends `DeleteCache` of the project. When `.fearless_out` holds `_unfinished.txt` before content assist reads the files, or, after content assist reads the files, holds `_unfinished.txt` or no longer holds a `<pkg>.json` that content assist read, a missing `<pkg>.txt` and a type or a method missing from the documentation are not errors: content assist shows its proposals without documentation. In a project that is 'invalid', content assist reads no rank file: no map directive and no `use` directive.

Content assist proposes:
- nothing while the cursor is inside a comment or a string, or touches a name that follows no dot (`ps|`, `Perso|`), except a type name being typed after `Name:` in a declaration;
- after a dot, or after a space following an expression, the methods of the type of the expression before the cursor, dot methods and operators alike: dot methods first, then by name and number of parameters; every method is proposed, also several with the same name and number of parameters (methods differing in the reference capability of the receiver). A method name being typed (`.fl|`, `+|`) keeps the methods starting with that name and is replaced by the chosen method; otherwise the chosen method replaces the dot, or is inserted at the cursor. An operator is inserted with a space on each side;
- after a lowercase name and a dot (`base.|`), and inside a qualified type name being typed (`base.Li|`, in code or in a `use` directive), the public types of the compiled package that name stands for whose simple name starts with what follows the dot.
- after `Name:` in a declaration, and while a simple type name is being typed there, the type expected at the cursor and the types without generics having that type as a direct supertype (the public types of the compiled packages, and the declarations of the edited file), whose simple name starts with what is typed.

When content assist has nothing to propose, content assist shows no popup.

After `(` and `,`, and on request, content assist also shows the type expected at the cursor as a hint, like `expected: Nat`. The type expected at the cursor is the type of the parameter of the argument of the call holding the cursor, or the result type of the method whose body holds the cursor.

Each proposal shows the signature of the method or type, with simple type names and with reference capabilities, and the documentation of the method or type.

The types of content assist are a best effort, not the typing of the compiler: the rules below say what content assist infers at least.
The text is read with the tokens of the compiler. The expression before the cursor is what follows the last `;`, `,`, `->`, `:` or the `'` starting a self name in the innermost bracket around the cursor, read with the grammar of the compiler: an atom, then posts left to right, where a post without parenthesis takes one atom, and `.m x = e rest` binds `x`. A bracket not closed yet runs to the end of the text. What the compiler would reject is unknown, and unknown has no methods. The type of:
- a type name is that type: a simple name is the alias of a `use` directive of the rank file of the package, else a type of the package; a name without `[..]` has no generics. A package name written in the package, in a qualified name or a `use` directive, stands for the package the `map` directives of the project give that name there. A number or a string is a `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float` or `base.Str`;
- a name is the type of what binds the name. The literals around the cursor bind the self name of the literal, or `this` for a top level declaration, the generics of a declaration and of the method holding the cursor, and the parameters of that method: to the written type, else to the type of the parameter of the method the literal implements (the method of that name, or, for a method without name, the only abstract method with that number of parameters not implemented by name in the literal). An earlier `x = e` of the same expression binds `x` to the first parameter of the continuation of the method of that `x = e`; inside that `e`, `x` is unknown;
- a literal is the type the literal declares, or the type written before the literal, or else the type expected by the call the literal is an argument of, or the result type of the method the literal is the whole body of; the generics of that type still open are bound by the types of the bodies of the methods of the literal;
- a call is the result types of every method of that name and number of arguments of the type of the receiver of the call (several when those methods differ in the reference capability of the receiver, or when the supertypes of a type the last compilation does not know disagree), each result type with the unknown generics, of the method and of the type of the receiver, bound by what the call shows: the explicit type arguments, the arguments meeting the types of the parameters, and the type expected of the call. An expression of several types has the methods of all those types.

A type declared in the edited file that the last compilation does not know has every method of the supertypes of that type: when supertypes have methods with the same name and number of parameters, all those methods are proposed.

## Implementation notes

- Output links in Eclipse: a stack frame of the runtime reads `error line: N in file F`; a compile error reads `In file: fear:/F` and, on a later line, the numbered source line `N| ` with a line of `^` under the source line (the lines before the source line are context). `F` is relative to the project folder. The Output view fills in by pieces, so the line of a compile error is looked up when the link is used. The Output view shows a `TextConsole` in a `TextConsoleViewer`, and links text through an `IPatternMatchListener` of that console.
- Hidden buttons in Eclipse: the extension point `org.eclipse.ui.perspectiveExtensions` hides toolbar items (`hiddenToolBarItem`) and menus (`hiddenMenuItem`) by id in one perspective. The ids are those of the buttons the Eclipse IDE package shows; Window > Perspective > Customize Perspective hides items by hand, and the workbench model saved at exit lists the ids of those items. The hidden items of `perspectiveExtensions` apply only when Eclipse makes the Fearless perspective from its factory: a Fearless perspective saved in the workspace keeps its own items. So the plugin also hides the Run and Debug toolbar actions, the action sets `org.eclipse.debug.ui.launchActionSet` and `org.eclipse.ui.externaltools.ExternalToolsSet`, with `IWorkbenchPage.hideActionSet` each time the Fearless perspective becomes active.
- Syntax highlighting in Eclipse: `org.eclipse.tm4e` colours a file from a TextMate grammar. The only grammar of Fearless is `syntaxes/fearless.tmLanguage.json` of the plugin, with the themes `fearless-light.css` and `fearless-dark.css`; a rewrite keeps those files, or writes the grammar again from the tokens of the Frontend.
- JUnit view in Eclipse: `JUnitCore.importTestRunSession` of `org.eclipse.jdt.junit.core` shows a JUnit XML report file.
- Build in Eclipse is the incremental build of Eclipse. Eclipse turns the build of a project Eclipse never built, and the build after a Clean, into a full build; a full build sends nothing, so the first Build of a project Eclipse never built sends nothing.
- The mirror of a registered project is the workspace project `fearless_<name>`, at the default location of Eclipse, holding the project folder as the linked folder `src`. New Folder and New File outside `src` warn that the folder or file is outside the folder of the project, and create it anyway in the workspace.
- Eclipse waits for the end of each manager process (see Starting the 'single' manager in ControllersDesign) that Eclipse starts, at most 10 seconds: a manager process that becomes the 'single' manager does not end, and that manager process writes its message files before taking the lock of `instance.lock`.
